1
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:06,514
What is rebase?

2
00:00:07,067 --> 00:00:13,379
Rebase replays your branch's commits on top of another commit, as if you had started your work later.

3
00:00:13,379 --> 00:00:18,879
The result is a straight history, but the replayed commits are new commits with new IDs.

4
00:00:18,879 --> 00:00:22,691
Understanding that one fact explains every rule about rebase.

5
00:00:24,433 --> 00:00:28,833
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

6
00:00:29,367 --> 00:00:33,186
Remember the branch's commit ID, then rebase:

7
00:00:33,733 --> 00:00:35,110
A straight line:

8
00:00:35,110 --> 00:00:39,583
"Add green tea" now comes after "Add opening hours".

9
00:00:39,583 --> 00:00:42,508
Same message, same change, new ID.

10
00:00:43,067 --> 00:00:44,443
A straight line:

11
00:00:44,443 --> 00:00:48,916
"Add green tea" now comes after "Add opening hours".

12
00:00:48,916 --> 00:00:51,841
Same message, same change, new ID.

13
00:00:53,600 --> 00:00:55,311
Let me try something.

14
00:00:55,311 --> 00:00:59,709
Rebase a branch that was already pushed, then push it:

15
00:01:00,267 --> 00:01:06,904
The server has the old feature-tea (with the old commit); your rebased branch does not contain it,

16
00:01:06,904 --> 00:01:10,291
so the push is not a fast-forward and is rejected.

17
00:01:10,291 --> 00:01:14,354
Rebase rewrote history that someone else might already have.

18
00:01:14,354 --> 00:01:15,438
The golden rule:

19
00:01:15,438 --> 00:01:18,621
never rebase commits that others have built on.

20
00:01:20,367 --> 00:01:25,119
It is Ada's own feature branch, nobody else works on it:

21
00:01:25,119 --> 00:01:26,731
replace it, safely.

22
00:01:28,467 --> 00:01:29,180
Got it.

23
00:01:29,180 --> 00:01:31,931
What do I need to remember?

24
00:01:32,467 --> 00:01:35,917
Rebase replays commits on a new base:

25
00:01:35,917 --> 00:01:39,181
same changes, new commits, new IDs.

26
00:01:39,733 --> 00:01:42,588
Linear history, but rewritten history:

27
00:01:42,588 --> 00:01:45,518
never rebase what others have built on.

28
00:01:46,067 --> 00:01:48,813
After rebasing your own pushed branch:

29
00:01:48,813 --> 00:01:50,836
git push --force-with-lease.

30
00:01:52,600 --> 00:01:55,753
I keep hearing about basic rebase.

31
00:01:55,753 --> 00:01:57,514
What is it, really?

32
00:01:58,067 --> 00:01:59,335
The everyday rebase:

33
00:01:59,335 --> 00:02:04,347
bring your feature branch up to date with main before opening or updating a PR,

34
00:02:04,347 --> 00:02:09,421
and git pull --rebase to keep your local commits on top of your teammates' work.

35
00:02:11,167 --> 00:02:15,566
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

36
