Update the feature branch with main's latest commit:

37
00:02:19,570 --> 00:02:23,507
Both feature commits are now on top of "Add opening hours".

38
00:02:23,507 --> 00:02:26,109
main can take them with a fast-forward:

39
00:02:26,667 --> 00:02:30,899
Both feature commits are now on top of "Add opening hours".

40
00:02:30,899 --> 00:02:33,696
main can take them with a fast-forward:

41
00:02:35,433 --> 00:02:37,042
Let me try something.

42
00:02:37,042 --> 00:02:40,643
Rebase with uncommitted changes in your folder:

43
00:02:41,200 --> 00:02:42,192
cannot rebase:

44
00:02:42,192 --> 00:02:44,105
You have unstaged changes.:

45
00:02:44,105 --> 00:02:50,978
rebase rewrites the working directory commit by commit; it refuses to risk your uncommitted work.

46
00:02:50,978 --> 00:02:54,380
git status shows the draft change in prices.txt.

47
00:02:56,133 --> 00:02:59,608
Let Git stash and restore it around the rebase:

48
00:03:01,367 --> 00:03:02,080
Got it.

49
00:03:02,080 --> 00:03:04,831
What do I need to remember?

50
00:03:05,367 --> 00:03:09,839
git rebase main puts your branch's commits on top of main:

51
00:03:09,839 --> 00:03:11,766
linear, fast-forwardable.

52
00:03:12,300 --> 00:03:15,935
git pull --rebase does the same against the upstream.

53
00:03:16,500 --> 00:03:20,350
Commit or --autostash uncommitted work before rebasing.

54
00:03:22,100 --> 00:03:24,939
How does rebase vs merge work?

55
00:03:25,500 --> 00:03:30,284
Merge and rebase both integrate work; they produce different histories.

56
00:03:30,284 --> 00:03:37,695
We build both from the same starting point, compare them, and see the one situation where rebase really hurts:

57
00:03:37,695 --> 00:03:40,659
rebasing a branch someone else has built on.

58
00:03:42,400 --> 00:03:46,799
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

59
00:03:47,333 --> 00:03:47,935
Merge:

60
00:03:47,935 --> 00:03:51,648
Rebase (then fast-forward main-side):

61
00:03:52,200 --> 00:03:55,179
Rebase (then fast-forward main-side):

62
00:03:55,179 --> 00:03:57,755
Same content, different history:

63
00:03:58,300 --> 00:04:01,410
Same content, different history:

64
00:04:03,167 --> 00:04:04,873
Let me try something.

65
00:04:04,873 --> 00:04:11,211
Ada pushes feature-tea; Grace builds on it; Ada then rebases and force-pushes.

66
00:04:11,767 --> 00:04:17,860
"Add green tea to the menu" and "Price green tea" now appear twice in Grace's history:

67
00:04:17,860 --> 00:04:24,661
the originals (which Grace's commit is built on) and the rebased copies (from Ada's force push),

68
00:04:24,661 --> 00:04:25,936
joined by a merge.

69
00:04:25,936 --> 00:04:29,266
Merged into main, the history would carry both.

70
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:36,491
Grace undoes her merge and rebases her own commit onto the new branch instead.

71
00:04:36,491 --> 00:04:42,545
Git recognises that the old copies are already upstream (same patches) and skips them:

72
00:04:44,300 --> 00:04:45,013
Got it.

73
00:04:45,013 --> 00:04:47,764
What do I need to remember?

74
00:04:48,300 --> 00:04:53,660
Merge records history as it happened; rebase rewrites it into a straight line.

75
00:04:54,200 --> 00:04:57,704
Same final files, different graphs.

76
00:04:58,267 --> 00:05:02,831
Rebasing a shared branch duplicates commits for everyone who built on it.

77
00:05:04,567 --> 00:05:07,306
Why would I need interactive rebase?

78
00:05:07,867 --> 00:05:15,218
git rebase -i opens a to-do list of your commits, one per line, and lets you decide what happens to each:

79
00:05:15,218 --> 00:05:19,560
keep, edit the message, stop to change it, combine, or delete.

80
00:05:19,560 --> 00:05:26,281
It is how you turn a messy work-in-progress branch into a clean series of commits before review.

81
00:05:28,033 --> 00:05:32,433
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

82
