00:06:17,500 --> 00:06:19,050
Let me try something.

92
00:06:19,050 --> 00:06:24,659
Two quick commits to clean up, then squash the first line of the to-do list:

93
00:06:25,200 --> 00:06:28,037
cannot 'squash' without a previous commit:

94
00:06:28,037 --> 00:06:34,925
squash and fixup meld a commit into the one above it in the list; the first line has nothing above it.

95
00:06:34,925 --> 00:06:37,830
Git refuses the list before doing anything:

96
00:06:37,830 --> 00:06:41,206
the rebase is in progress with "No commands done".

97
00:06:41,206 --> 00:06:46,610
Fix the list with git rebase --edit-todo (then git rebase --continue), or abort:

98
00:06:48,367 --> 00:06:50,814
Include the commit to meld into:

99
00:06:50,814 --> 00:06:56,396
HEAD~2, and fixup the second line; then give the result a proper message.

100
00:06:58,133 --> 00:06:58,847
Got it.

101
00:06:58,847 --> 00:07:01,598
What do I need to remember?

102
00:07:02,133 --> 00:07:09,023
git rebase -i BASE lists the commits after BASE; edit the verbs, save, Git replays.

103
00:07:09,567 --> 00:07:17,106
pick, reword, edit, squash, fixup, drop; reorder by moving lines.

104
00:07:17,667 --> 00:07:23,386
Only on commits you have not shared (or your own branch, then --force-with-lease).

105
00:07:25,133 --> 00:07:28,238
What should I know about rebase conflicts?

106
00:07:28,800 --> 00:07:34,775
A rebase replays commits one at a time, so a conflict can happen at each step.

107
00:07:34,775 --> 00:07:38,835
We create one on purpose and resolve it step by step:

108
00:07:38,835 --> 00:07:43,584
read the status, fix the file, git add, git rebase --continue.

109
00:07:45,333 --> 00:07:49,733
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

