Recreate the conflict (ORIG_HEAD is the branch before the rebase),

118
00:08:27,564 --> 00:08:30,985
edit the file, and continue without git add:

119
00:08:31,533 --> 00:08:37,800
Git does not look at the file content to decide whether you are done; it looks at the index.

120
00:08:37,800 --> 00:08:42,023
Until git add marks the file resolved, it is still "unmerged":

121
00:08:43,767 --> 00:08:45,566
Here is the fix.

122
00:08:47,300 --> 00:08:48,013
Got it.

123
00:08:48,013 --> 00:08:50,764
What do I need to remember?

124
00:08:51,300 --> 00:08:56,290
A rebase stops at each conflicting commit; git status says which one.

125
00:08:56,833 --> 00:09:03,058
Fix → git add → git rebase --continue; --skip drops the commit, --abort cancels.

126
00:09:03,600 --> 00:09:08,055
During a rebase, "ours" is the new base and "theirs" is your commit.

127
00:09:09,800 --> 00:09:12,495
Can you show me aborting a rebase?

128
00:09:13,033 --> 00:09:19,724
git rebase --abort cancels a rebase in progress and puts the branch back exactly where it was before,

129
00:09:19,724 --> 00:09:22,572
however many commits were already replayed.

130
00:09:22,572 --> 00:09:26,348
It is the emergency exit that makes rebasing safe to try.

131
00:09:28,100 --> 00:09:32,500
